# Approved Build Protection

- Treat behavior already approved and committed as a compatibility contract. Preserve it by default; do not remove, replace, disable, or silently narrow it when adding features or data.
- Inspect the current behavior and regression coverage before editing an existing product path. Prefer additive changes and keep prior routes, data, IDs, and user actions available.
- For every completed, coherent user-approved work batch, run the relevant regression checks, review the staged diff, and create a commit checkpoint before starting the next batch. A “go on” request continues from that checkpoint; it does not authorize replacing or dropping earlier work. Stage only files for the current batch, and preserve unrelated or in-progress user changes.
- Never delete, overwrite, hide, or silently reclassify an approved directory listing, route, feature, or user action. Intentional removals or behavior changes require explicit user authorization, a written reason, a migration/rollback path, and regression coverage for both retained and changed behavior.
- Never amend or rebase commits already on `main`, and never force-push shared release branches.
- Changes to existing product files require the `approved-behavior-change` PR label, one approving review from someone other than the author, and passing `approved-build-protection` CI. The label authorizes review of the diff; it does not by itself authorize a behavior change.
- Before any merge or deployment, confirm the PR base is the intended release branch, the diff contains only the intended work, all required checks pass, and the branch protection actually requires the review and status check. A local commit or successful build is not a deployment.
- Do not merge or deploy around a failed, missing, stale, or non-required check.
